7.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {Colleagues 13, 16, 9, and 21 have identified the decisive first-order mechanism: persistent volatility drag on the compound-growth path of B. With 20 % weekly moves, the annualized volatility exceeds 140 %; even under a linear ARIMA-with-drift specification the geometric (log-price) growth rate is therefore reduced by roughly ½σ² each year. Over thirty years that shortfall shifts the entire unconditional distribution of terminal wealth materially below the deterministic 10 % path of A, before any liquidation occurs.  

The one-year optimal-liquidation window cannot restore this gap. Optional-sampling arguments correctly note that E[max_{0≤s≤T} X_s] exceeds E[X_T] for a drifted random walk, yet that inequality is evaluated around an already downward-shifted mean. On paths that finish deep in the left tail after three decades, a single year of trading is insufficient to escape the drawdown entirely, especially once realistic bid–ask and market-impact costs are acknowledged. Consequently the ex-ante expected proceeds under the admissible liquidation rule remain lower for B than the certain terminal value delivered by A.  

The shared arithmetic drift therefore does not imply equal expectations once multiplicative compounding and the finite liquidation window are taken into account. Claims that the timing option dominates overlook the relative magnitudes: the drag term compounds for thirty years while the option applies for only one. This leaves A as the strongest true statement among the listed choices.}

