8.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Round 2 sharpens the arithmetic vs. geometric split. G camp (1,3,4,6,8,10-12,15,18-20) reiterates that problem language ("linear growth trend," "random walk around a mean with drift," ARIMA-with-drift on price level, constant autocovariance) fixes an arithmetic process; unconditional E[terminal] at any fixed date is therefore identical, and the sole asymmetry is the year-31 optimal-liquidation window. For B, optional-sampling on a drifted RW yields E[max_{0≤s≤T} X_s] > E[X_T] (T=1 yr, σ>0) under any admissible stopping rule; A supplies zero incremental value. Hence G is the strongest true claim. Positive drift rules out all martingale statements; randomness rules out E/H; geometric-drag arguments presuppose an unstated multiplicative specification.

A camp (2,5,7,9,13,14,16,17,21) counters that 20 % weekly moves (persistent, autocorrelated) generate annualized σ ≫ 100 %, so ½σ² drag compounds multiplicatively over 30 years and shifts B’s entire unconditional terminal distribution materially below A’s deterministic P₀(1.1)^30 before the window opens; the one-year interval cannot offset accumulated left-tail drawdowns (especially once bid–ask/market-impact costs are admitted) and the timing premium is therefore second-order. A is strongest true.

Preserved from earlier: arithmetic wording is decisive; liquidation option is first-order under the stated model; no pathwise dominance; both processes carry drift. My lean unchanged: G strongest under explicit linear/ARIMA specification; geometric arguments require an assumption the problem does not supply. Track any later clarification on price negativity or compounding convention.}

